I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, diffraction profile, and available crystallography utilities first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build a multiphase refinement, validate the fit and phase fractions, and export the requested CIFs, profile, report, JSON, and final GSAS-II project.

The chemistry points strongly toward copper tungstate formation, but Cu–W–O has several plausible structures and precursor residues can coexist. I’m now extracting the peak set and checking it against the local crystallographic search databases before committing to a phase model.

The observed peak sequence matches triclinic CuWO₄ (wolframite-type, \(P\bar1\)) across the full angular range. The apparent offset is nearly uniform (~+0.30° 2θ), so I’m treating it as an instrumental zero/sample-position term and testing whether any statistically meaningful residual peaks remain after a full-pattern refinement.

The single-phase CuWO₄ model converged to \(R_{wp}\approx8.1\%\) and \(R_F^2\approx6.0\%\), with a refined triclinic cell near \(a=4.7110\), \(b=5.8455\), \(c=4.8839\) Å. I’m cleaning up parameter correlations (absolute histogram scale versus phase scale, and instrumental versus sample broadening) and checking the difference curve specifically at CuO and WO₃ diagnostic reflections.

A weak but coherent second phase is present: ambient monoclinic WO₃ (\(P2_1/c\)). Adding it lowers \(R_{wp}\) from 8.09% to 7.55%, refines to a physically correct WO₃ cell (\(a=7.3033\), \(b=7.5459\), \(c=7.6896\) Å, \(\beta=90.873^\circ\)), and gives about 2.27 wt%. CuO instead refines to the lower bound, so I’m excluding it from the modeled crystalline phases.

I’m comparing a small set of controlled refinements—axial-divergence profile, background flexibility, preferred orientation, and CuWO₄ coordinate relaxation—against the same two-phase baseline. This is to keep only improvements that are both statistically meaningful and physically supportable.

The model-comparison results favor a restrained final refinement: two phases, refined cells and scales, a mild Cu-site coordinate adjustment in CuWO₄, and a smooth background. Oxygen coordinates and all trace-WO₃ atomic coordinates will remain fixed to reference structures because the laboratory X-ray data do not determine them reliably. I’m now rebuilding that model cleanly as `final.gpx` and exporting all deliverables from it.

The clean final refinement has converged with no SVD singularity: \(R_{wp}=7.377\%\), \(R_p=5.357\%\), and GOF 3.038 under Poisson weighting. The normalized crystalline fractions are 97.852 wt% CuWO₄ and 2.148 wt% WO₃ (formal scale-derived s.u. 0.063 wt% each). I’m doing the final consistency checks on the CIFs, project, profile arithmetic, and JSON before writing the concise uncertainty report.

Identified two crystalline phases:

- CuWO₄, triclinic \(P\bar1\): 97.852 wt%
- WO₃, monoclinic \(P2_1/n\): 2.148 wt%

